  B.push({ box: { title: "Exam allocation you must know", body: "Chapter 3 = **7 questions / 7 points** of 40 questions and 40 points. In the official Sample Exam these are questions **#11 to #17**. **PTFL-3.2 receives three questions — more than any objective outside Chapter 4.** The two K4 questions (#16, #17) are the first scenario-based items on the paper and both use the **regional patient health-records** scenario." } });
  B.push({ box: { kind: "warn", title: "The one thing that decides this chapter", body: "**Three of the seven marks come from one table: which performance risks belong to which architecture.** The official exam asks it three times in a row, twice in a \"_consider the following technical environments… which of these is most likely to have a risk due to X_\" multiple-selection format. **Nine architectures, each with two or three named risks.** Build that table, rebuild it from blank paper, and you have taken 43% of the chapter." } });

  // --- PTFL-3.1.1 ---
  B.push({ h2: "PTFL-3.1.1 (K2) — Principal Performance Testing Activities" });
  B.push({ box: { title: "Original syllabus statement", body: "Understand the principal performance testing activities." } });
  B.push({ p: "**The framing sentence.** \"_Performance testing is **iterative in nature**. **Each test provides valuable insights** into application and system performance. The information gathered from one test is used to **correct or optimize application and system parameters**. The next test iteration will then **show the results of modifications**, and so on **until test objectives are reached**._\"" });
  B.push({ p: "**The alignment sentence.** \"_Performance testing activities **align with the ISTQB test process**._\" That means the seven activities below are the standard Foundation-Level test process activities, seen through a performance lens. The exam asks **which activity owns a named task** — so the discriminator is always the task, never the definition of the activity." });
  B.push({ table: { title: "The seven activities — and what belongs to each", head: ["Activity", "What the syllabus places here"], widths: [3.4, 10.6], rows: [
    ["**Test planning**", "**Particularly important for performance testing** because of the need for the **allocation of test environments, test data, tools and human resources**. This is the activity in which **the scope of performance testing is established**. **During test planning, risk identification and risk analysis activities are completed** and relevant information is updated in test planning documentation (e.g., **test plan, level test plan**). **Just as test planning is revisited and modified as needed, so are risks, risk levels and risk status modified to reflect changes in risk conditions.**"],
    ["**Test monitoring and control**", "**Control measures are defined to provide action plans should issues be encountered** which might impact performance efficiency: **increasing the load generation capacity** if the infrastructure does not generate the desired loads · **changed, new or replaced hardware** · **changes to network components** · **changes to software implementation**. **The performance test objectives are evaluated to check for exit criteria achievement.**"],
    ["**Test analysis**", "**Effective performance tests are based on an analysis of performance requirements, test objectives, Service Level Agreements (SLA), IT architecture, process models and other items that comprise the test basis.** May be supported by **modeling and analysis of system resource requirements and/or behavior using spreadsheets or capacity planning tools**. **Specific test conditions are identified** such as **load levels, timing conditions, and transactions to be tested**. **The required type(s) of performance test (e.g., load, stress, scalability) are then decided.**"],
    ["**Test design**", "**Performance test cases are designed.** These are **generally created in modular form** so that they may be used as the **building blocks of larger, more complex performance tests** (see §4.2)."],
    ["**Test implementation**", "**Performance test cases are ordered into performance test procedures.** These procedures should **reflect the steps normally taken by the user and other functional activities** to be covered. A test implementation activity is **establishing and/or resetting the test environment before each test execution**. Since performance testing is **typically data-driven**, a process is needed to **establish test data that is representative of actual production data in volume and type** so that **production use can be simulated**."],
    ["**Test execution**", "**Test execution occurs when the performance test is conducted, often by using performance test tools.** **Test results are evaluated to determine if the system's performance meets the requirements and other stated objectives. Any defects are reported.**"],
    ["**Test completion**", "**Performance test results are provided to the stakeholders** (e.g., **architects, managers, product owners**) in a **test summary report**. The results are **expressed through metrics which are often aggregated to simplify the meaning** of the test results. **Visual means of reporting such as dashboards** are often used. At the close of a defined period, a **point of test closure** may be reached where **designed tests, test tool assets (test cases and test procedures), test data and other testware are archived or passed on to other testers for later use during system maintenance activities**."],
  ] } });
  B.push({ box: { kind: "warn", title: "The two official questions, and the exact tasks they turn on", body: "**#11 — \"When should risk identification and analysis occur?\" → Test planning.** The official rationale: **risk identification and analysis is done at the test planning stage. This information is then used for the analysis and design of the tests.** Analysis and design, implementation and execution, and closure are all **too late in the process**.  **#12 — \"When should the test cases be ordered into performance test procedures?\" → Test implementation and execution.** The rationale: **test planning and test analysis and design are when the test cases are planned and designed**; **test closure is too late**. Note the option wording pairs activities — _test implementation and execution_ is offered as one option." } });
  B.push({ p: "**The ongoing-activity sentence.** \"_Performance testing is often considered to be an **ongoing activity** in that it is **performed at multiple times and at all test levels (component, integration, system, system integration and acceptance testing)**._\" This is the bridge back to **PTFL-1.3.1** and forward to **PTFL-3.4.1**." });
  B.push({ p: "**Common misunderstanding.** Reading \"test analysis\" as the place where risks are identified. Risks are identified and analysed in **test planning**; test **analysis** is where the **test basis** is analysed and **test conditions** — load levels, timing conditions, transactions — are identified, and where the **type of performance test is decided**." });
  B.push({ lead: "Related concepts: iterative testing, ISTQB test process, test planning, risk identification, risk analysis, test plan, level test plan, test monitoring and control, control measures, exit criteria, test analysis, test basis, SLA, capacity planning tools, test conditions, test design, modular test cases, test implementation, test procedures, data-driven testing, test execution, test completion, test summary report, dashboards, test closure, testware archiving." });

  // --- PTFL-3.2.1 ---
  B.push({ h2: "PTFL-3.2.1 (K2) — Categories of Performance Risks for Different Architectures" });
  B.push({ box: { title: "Original syllabus statement", body: "Explain typical categories of performance risks for different architectures." } });
  B.push({ p: "**The framing.** \"_Application or system performance **varies considerably based on the architecture, application and host environment**. While **it is not possible to provide a complete list of performance risks for all systems**, the list below includes **some typical types of risks associated with particular architectures**._\" Nine architectures are named. **Three of your seven chapter marks are decided here.**" });
  B.push({ table: { title: "The nine architectures and their named risks", head: ["Architecture", "Definition", "Performance risks named by the syllabus"], widths: [2.6, 4.4, 7.0], rows: [
    ["**Single computer systems**", "Systems or applications that run **entirely on one non-virtualized computer**.", "**Excessive resource consumption** including **memory leaks, background activities such as security software, slow storage subsystems** (low-speed external devices or **disk fragmentation**), and **operating system mismanagement**. **Inefficient implementation of algorithms** which **do not make use of available resources (e.g., main memory)** and so **execute slower than required**."],
    ["**Multi-tier systems**", "**Systems of systems that run on multiple servers**, each performing a specific set of tasks — **database server, application server, presentation server**. Each server is itself a computer and **subject to the single-computer risks**.", "**Poor or non-scalable database design · network bottlenecks · inadequate bandwidth or capacity on any single server.**"],
    ["**Distributed systems**", "Similar to multi-tier, but **the various servers may change dynamically** — e.g., an e-commerce system accessing **different inventory databases depending on the geographic location** of the person ordering. **Inherits all multi-tier risks.**", "**Critical workflows or dataflows to, from, or through unreliable or unpredictable remote servers**, especially when such servers suffer **periodic connection problems or intermittent periods of intense load**."],
    ["**Virtualized systems**", "Systems where the **physical hardware hosts multiple virtual computers**. These VMs may host single-computer systems and applications as well as servers that are part of a multi-tier or distributed architecture.", "**Excessive load on the hardware across all the virtual machines**; **improper configuration of the host virtual machine resulting in inadequate resources**."],
    ["**Dynamic / cloud-based systems**", "Systems that **offer the ability to scale on demand**, increasing capacity as load increases. **Typically distributed and virtualized multi-tier systems**, with **self-scaling features designed specifically to mitigate some of the performance risks** of those architectures.", "**Failures to properly configure these features during initial setup or subsequent updates.**"],
    ["**Client-server systems**", "Systems running on a client that **communicate via a user interface with a single server, multi-tier server, or distributed server**. **Since there is code running on the client, the single-computer risks apply to that code**, while the server-side issues apply as well.", "**Connection speed and reliability issues · network congestion at the client connection point (e.g., public Wi-Fi) · potential problems due to firewalls, packet inspection and server load balancing.**"],
    ["**Mobile applications**", "Applications running on a **smartphone, tablet, or other mobile device**. Subject to the risks of **client-server and browser-based (web apps)** applications.", "**Limited and variable resources and connectivity** — affected by **location, battery life, charge state, available memory on the device and temperature**. For apps using **device sensors or radios such as accelerometers or Bluetooth**, **slow dataflows from those sources**. **Heavy interactions with other local mobile apps and remote web services**, any of which can become a **performance efficiency bottleneck**."],
    ["**Embedded real-time systems**", "Systems that **work within or even control everyday things** — cars (entertainment systems, **intelligent braking systems**), **elevators, traffic signals, HVAC systems**.", "**Many of the risks of mobile devices**, including **(increasingly) connectivity-related issues** since these devices are connected to the Internet. **However** — the diminished performance of a mobile video game is usually **not a safety hazard**, while such slowdowns in a **vehicle braking system could prove catastrophic**."],
    ["**Mainframe applications**", "**In many cases decades-old applications** supporting **mission-critical business functions in a data center, sometimes via batch processing**. **Most are quite predictable and fast when used as originally designed.**", "**Many are now accessible via APIs, web services, or through their database**, which can result in **unexpected loads that affect throughput of established applications**."],
  ] } });
  B.push({ box: { kind: "warn", title: "The two multiple-selection questions, decoded", body: "**#13 — which environments are most likely to have a risk due to connectivity issues? Answer: mobile and embedded.** The official rationale: **mobile and embedded environments are particularly prone to connectivity issues, particularly when embedded software is used in IoT devices**; the other environments **are not prone to connection dropping**.  **#14 — which are most likely to have a risk due to memory leaks? Answer: virtualized, client/server and browser-based, mobile, embedded.** The reasoning is given per environment: **virtualized uses shared resources across different applications, including memory**; **dynamic/cloud-based is designed to dynamically scale, so while memory leaks may occur they are less of a risk because the environment will scale to compensate**; **client/server and browser-based are prone to memory leaks, particularly since a lot of this code is written in C and C++**; **mobile is prone to leakage and particularly at risk due to the limited available memory**; **embedded likewise, and these also tend to be written in languages that have less protection against leaks**; **mainframes tend to run older code, often written in the higher level languages that provide their own memory management**." } });
  B.push({ p: "**Architectures combine.** \"_Note that **any particular application or system may incorporate two or more of the architectures listed above**, which means that **all relevant risks will apply**. In fact, given the **Internet of Things and the explosion of mobile applications** — two areas where **extreme levels of interaction and connection is the rule** — **it is possible that all architectures are present in some form in an application, and thus all risks can apply**._\"" });
  B.push({ box: { kind: "ok", title: "Risks that are not architectural — the third official question", body: "\"_While architecture is clearly an important technical decision with a profound impact on performance risks, **other technical decisions also influence and create risks**._\" The syllabus names three: **memory leaks are more common with languages that allow direct heap memory management, such as C and C++** · **performance issues are different for relational versus non-relational databases** · and such decisions **extend all the way down to the design of individual functions or methods (e.g., the choice of a recursive as opposed to an iterative algorithm)**. **Official question #15 is built entirely on the first of these:** if the software is written in **C++** rather than **Python**, you must monitor **memory use**, because **C and C++ require the programmer to do their own memory management, which sometimes results in memory leaks (where memory is allocated, but not de-allocated)**. **Network latency, connectivity and batch processing would have the same issues regardless of the language used.**" } });
  B.push({ p: "**A closing caution from the syllabus.** \"_As a tester, the ability to **know about or even influence such decisions will vary, depending on the roles and responsibilities of testers within the organization and software development lifecycle**._\"" });
  B.push({ lead: "Related concepts: single computer system, memory leak, background activity, disk fragmentation, operating system mismanagement, algorithm efficiency, multi-tier system, database server, application server, presentation server, network bottleneck, distributed system, remote server reliability, virtualized system, host virtual machine configuration, dynamic/cloud-based system, self-scaling, client-server system, public Wi-Fi congestion, firewall, packet inspection, load balancing, mobile application, battery life, charge state, temperature, accelerometer, Bluetooth, embedded real-time system, intelligent braking system, HVAC, safety hazard, mainframe application, batch processing, API exposure, heap memory management, C and C++, relational versus non-relational databases, recursive versus iterative algorithms." });

  // --- PTFL-3.3.1 ---
  B.push({ h2: "PTFL-3.3.1 (K4) — Performance Risks Across the Software Development Lifecycle" });
  B.push({ box: { title: "Original syllabus statement", body: "Analyze performance risks for a given product across the software development lifecycle." } });
  B.push({ p: "**This is the first K4 objective on the paper.** K4 means **analyze** — the candidate can **separate information related to a procedure or technique into its constituent parts for better understanding and can distinguish between facts and inferences**. In practice the question gives you a project description and asks **when** risk analysis should happen." });
  B.push({ table: { title: "The four-step performance risk process", head: ["#", "Step (syllabus wording)"], widths: [0.8, 13.2], rows: [
    ["1", "**Identify risks to product quality**, focusing on characteristics such as **time behavior, resource utilization, and capacity**."],
    ["2", "**Assess the identified risks**, ensuring that the **relevant architecture categories (see Section 3.2) are addressed**. **Evaluate the overall level of risk for each identified risk in terms of likelihood and impact using clearly defined criteria.**"],
    ["3", "**Take appropriate risk mitigation actions for each risk item** based on the **nature of the risk item and the level of risk**."],
    ["4", "**Manage risks on an ongoing basis** to ensure that the risks are **adequately mitigated prior to release**."],
  ] } });
  B.push({ p: "**Who takes part, and what each side must appreciate.** The participants \"_should include **both business and technical stakeholders**_\", and the syllabus is precise about what each group brings." });
  B.push({ table: { head: ["Stakeholder group", "Who must be included", "What they must appreciate"], widths: [2.6, 5.4, 6.0], rows: [
    ["**Business stakeholders**", "Those with **a particular awareness of how performance problems in production will actually affect customers, users, the business, and other downstream stakeholders**.", "That **intended usage, business-, societal-, or safety-criticality, potential financial and/or reputational damage, civil or criminal legal liability** and similar factors **affect risk from a business perspective, creating risks and influencing the impact of failures**."],
    ["**Technical stakeholders**", "Those with **a deep understanding of the performance implications of relevant requirements, architecture, design, and implementation decisions**.", "That **architecture, design, and implementation decisions affect performance risks from a technical perspective, creating risks and influencing the likelihood of defects**."],
  ] } });
  B.push({ box: { kind: "warn", title: "Impact versus likelihood — the split the exam relies on", body: "**Business stakeholders influence the IMPACT of failures. Technical stakeholders influence the LIKELIHOOD of defects.** That is the syllabus's own division, and it is the cleanest way to remember why both groups are mandatory. Step 2 evaluates risk **in terms of likelihood and impact using clearly defined criteria** — one side of that equation comes from each group." } });
  B.push({ p: "**Formality and timing.** \"_The specific risk analysis process chosen should have **the appropriate level of formality and rigor**. For performance-related risks, it is **especially important that the risk analysis process be started early and is repeated regularly**. In other words, **the tester should avoid relying entirely on performance testing conducted towards the end of the system test level and system integration test level**._\"" });
  B.push({ p: "**Why late is expensive.** \"_Many projects, especially **larger and more complex systems of systems projects**, have met with **unfortunate surprises due to the late discovery of performance defects which resulted from requirements, design, architecture, and implementation decisions made early in the project**. The emphasis should therefore be on an **iterative approach to performance risk identification, assessment, mitigation, and management throughout the software development lifecycle**._\"" });
  B.push({ box: { kind: "ok", title: "The worked example the syllabus gives", body: "\"_If **large volumes of data will be handled via a relational database**, the **slow performance of many-to-many joins due to poor database design** may **only reveal itself during dynamic testing with large-scale test datasets, such as those used during system test**. **However, a careful technical review that includes experienced database engineers can predict the problems prior to database implementation.** After such a review, **in an iterative approach, risks are identified and assessed again**._\" This is **PTFL-1.3.1's static testing argument** restated as a risk-management argument — and it is the reasoning behind the answer to official question #17." } });
  B.push({ p: "**Mitigation spans the whole process.** \"_Risk mitigation and management **must span and influence the entire software development process, not just dynamic testing**._\" The syllabus example: when **critical performance-related decisions such as the expected number of transactions or simultaneous users cannot be specified early**, it is important that **design and architecture decisions allow for highly variable scalability (e.g., on-demand cloud-based computing resources)**. **This enables early risk mitigation decisions to be made.**" });
  B.push({ p: "**Residual risk must be quantified.** \"_As with any type of quality risk, performance-related risks **can never be avoided completely** — i.e., **some risk of performance-related production failure will always exist**. Therefore, the risk management process **must include providing a realistic and specific evaluation of the residual level of risk** to the business and technical stakeholders involved._\" The syllabus then gives its own bad example and good example:" });
  B.push({ table: { head: ["Unhelpful residual-risk statement", "Helpful residual-risk statement"], widths: [7.0, 7.0], rows: [
    ["\"**Yes, it's still possible for customers to experience long delays during check out**\" — **not helpful, as it gives no idea of what amount of risk mitigation has occurred or of the level of risk that remains**", "**Providing clear insight into the percentage of customers likely to experience delays equal to or exceeding certain thresholds** — this **helps people understand the status**"],
  ] } });
  B.push({ p: "**Exam focus.** One **K4** question. The official version presents the **regional patient health-records** scenario — millions of records, doctors in offices, hospitals and urgent care facilities, **information presented within three seconds**, **particularly for patients with critical allergies and preconditions** — and asks **when is the best time in the project to analyze and assess the performance risks?** The answer is **repeatedly throughout the requirements, development and performance testing**. The rationale: **since performance is critical, the risks must be analyzed as early as possible and then repeatedly as the system is assembled, since risks may change both in impact and likelihood**. Each distractor **leaves out a phase**: one **ignores looking at the risks during development**; one **leaves out the requirements and development**; one **leaves out the requirements and development options for analysis**." });
  B.push({ box: { kind: "warn", title: "The K4 answer pattern for PTFL-3.3.1", body: "**When a performance-risk timing question offers one option that spans the whole lifecycle and three that name only some phases, the spanning option wins.** The syllabus sentence behind it is: **started early and repeated regularly**, with an **iterative approach to identification, assessment, mitigation and management throughout the software development lifecycle**. Also note the reason risks must be **re-assessed**, not merely re-checked: **risks may change both in impact and likelihood**." } });
  B.push({ lead: "Related concepts: quality risk analysis, risk identification, risk assessment, likelihood, impact, risk mitigation, ongoing risk management, residual risk, business stakeholders, technical stakeholders, safety-criticality, reputational damage, legal liability, formality and rigor, systems of systems, many-to-many joins, technical review, on-demand cloud-based computing, thresholds." });

  // --- PTFL-3.4.1 ---
  B.push({ h2: "PTFL-3.4.1 (K4) — Performance Testing Activities Across Lifecycle Models" });
  B.push({ box: { title: "Original syllabus statement", body: "Analyze a given project to determine the appropriate performance testing activities for each phase of the software development lifecycle." } });
  B.push({ p: "**The framing.** \"_Performance testing activities will be **organized and performed differently, depending on the type of software development lifecycle in use**._\" Three lifecycle families are covered: **sequential**, **iterative and incremental**, and **COTS / supplier-acquirer models**." });
  B.push({ h3: "Sequential development models" });
  B.push({ p: "\"_The **ideal practice** of performance testing in sequential development models is to **include performance criteria as a part of the acceptance criteria which are defined at the outset of a project**. Reinforcing the lifecycle view of testing, **performance testing activities should be conducted throughout the software development lifecycle**. As the project progresses, **each successive performance test activity should be based on items defined in the prior activities**._\" **Nine phases**, each with its own verb — and the verb is what the exam tests." });
  B.push({ table: { head: ["Phase", "Performance testing activity (syllabus wording)"], widths: [3.6, 10.4], rows: [
    ["**Concept**", "**Verify that system performance goals are defined as acceptance criteria for the project.**"],
    ["**Requirements**", "**Verify that performance requirements are defined and represent stakeholder needs correctly.**"],
    ["**Analysis and Design**", "**Verify that the system design reflects the performance requirements.**"],
    ["**Coding / Implementation**", "**Verify that the code is efficient and reflects the requirements and design in terms of performance.**"],
    ["**Component Testing**", "**Conduct component level performance testing.**"],
    ["**Component Integration Testing**", "**Conduct performance testing at the component integration level.**"],
    ["**System Testing**", "**Conduct performance testing at the system level**, which includes **hardware, software, procedures and data that are representative of the production environment**. **System interfaces may be simulated provided that they give a true representation of performance.**"],
    ["**System Integration Testing**", "**Conduct performance testing with the entire system which is representative of the production environment.**"],
    ["**Acceptance Testing**", "**Validate that system performance meets the originally stated user needs and acceptance criteria.**"],
  ] } });
  B.push({ box: { kind: "ok", title: "Verify versus validate — and where simulation is allowed", body: "The first four phases all say **verify**; the last says **validate**. **Verification checks the artefact against its predecessor; validation checks the system against the originally stated user needs and acceptance criteria.** Note also the single place the syllabus permits simulation: at **system testing**, **system interfaces may be simulated provided that they give a true representation of performance** — but at **system integration testing** the requirement is **the entire system which is representative of the production environment**." } });
  B.push({ h3: "Iterative and incremental development models" });
  B.push({ p: "\"_In these development models, such as **Agile**, performance testing is also seen as an **iterative and incremental activity**. Performance testing **can occur as part of the first iteration, or as an iteration dedicated entirely to performance testing**. However, with these lifecycle models, **the execution of performance testing may be performed by a separate team tasked with performance testing**._\"" });
  B.push({ p: "**Continuous Integration.** \"_CI is commonly performed in iterative and incremental software development lifecycles, which **facilitates a highly automated execution of tests**. **The most common objective of testing in CI is to perform regression testing and ensure each build is stable.** Performance testing **can be part of the automated tests performed in CI if the tests are designed in such a way as to be executed at a build level**. However, **unlike functional automated tests, there are additional concerns**_\" — four of them:" });
  B.push({ table: { head: ["CI concern", "What the syllabus says"], widths: [4.0, 10.0], rows: [
    ["**The setup of the performance test environment**", "**Often requires a test environment that is available on demand, such as a cloud-based performance test environment.**"],
    ["**Determining which performance tests to automate in CI**", "**Due to the short timeframe available for CI tests**, CI performance tests **may be a subset of more extensive performance tests conducted by a specialist team at other times during an iteration**."],
    ["**Creating the performance tests for CI**", "**The main objective of performance tests as part of CI is to ensure a change does not negatively impact performance.** **Depending on the changes made for any given build, new performance tests may be required.**"],
    ["**Executing performance tests on portions of an application or system**", "**Often requires the tools and test environments to be capable of rapid performance testing, including the ability to select subsets of applicable tests.**"],
  ] } });
  B.push({ p: "**The seven iterative lifecycle activities.** \"_Performance testing in the iterative and incremental software development lifecycles can also have **its own lifecycle activities**._\"" });
  B.push({ table: { head: ["Activity", "Performance testing content"], widths: [3.2, 10.8], rows: [
    ["**Release Planning**", "Performance testing is considered **from the perspective of all iterations in a release, from the first iteration to the final iteration**. **Performance risks are identified and assessed, and mitigation measures planned.** Often includes **planning of any final performance testing before the release of the application**."],
    ["**Iteration Planning**", "Performance testing **may be performed within the iteration and as each iteration is completed**. **Performance risks are assessed in more detail for each user story.**"],
    ["**User Story Creation**", "**User stories often form the basis of performance requirements in Agile methodologies**, with the **specific performance criteria described in the associated acceptance criteria**. These are referred to as **\"non-functional\" user stories**."],
    ["**Design of performance tests**", "**Performance requirements and criteria which are described in particular user stories are used for the design of tests** (see §4.2)."],
    ["**Coding / Implementation**", "**Performance testing may be performed at a component level** — for example, **the tuning of algorithms for optimum performance efficiency**."],
    ["**Testing / Evaluation**", "**While testing is typically performed in close proximity to development activities, performance testing may be performed as a separate activity**, depending on the **scope and objectives of performance testing during the iteration**. If the goal is to test **the iteration as a completed set of user stories**, a **wider scope** is needed than for a **single user story**. **This may be scheduled in a dedicated iteration for performance testing.**"],
    ["**Delivery**", "**Since delivery will introduce the application to the production environment, performance will need to be monitored to determine if the application achieves the desired levels of performance in actual usage.**"],
  ] } });
  B.push({ h3: "COTS and other supplier / acquirer models" });
  B.push({ p: "\"_Many organizations **do not develop applications and systems themselves**, but instead are in the position of **acquiring software from vendor sources or from open-source projects**. In such **supplier/acquirer models**, performance is an important consideration that **requires testing from both the supplier (vendor/developer) and acquirer (customer) perspectives**._\"" });
  B.push({ table: { head: ["Situation", "Where responsibility sits"], widths: [5.0, 9.0], rows: [
    ["**Regardless of the source of the application**", "**It is often the responsibility of the customer to validate that the performance meets their requirements.**"],
    ["**Customized vendor-developed software**", "**Performance requirements and associated acceptance criteria should be specified as part of the contract between the vendor and customer.**"],
    ["**COTS applications**", "**The customer has sole responsibility to test the performance of the product in a realistic test environment prior to deployment.**"],
  ] } });
  B.push({ box: { kind: "warn", title: "The official K4 question — and why the answer is a combination", body: "Question #17 uses the same **health-records** scenario and asks: **what is the best way to address the performance testing for the response time?** The answer: **conduct a technical review of the database implementation and conduct a performance test from the UI with the full database loaded.** The official rationale: **the best way to do the testing is to first do a technical analysis because this can be done at design time. Once implemented, full testing will be needed to verify that the solution does indeed deliver the performance required.** The distractors fail as follows: testing from the UI with the full data set **leaves out the static testing**; testing via the web services at API level **would bypass any issues that might occur at the UI end and doesn't consider the database loading**; and the network assessment option is wrong because **the network is less likely an issue regarding the data handling for such a large amount of data**." } });
  B.push({ box: { kind: "ok", title: "The K4 answer pattern for PTFL-3.4.1", body: "**When asked how to address a performance concern across a lifecycle, prefer the option that pairs an early STATIC activity with a later DYNAMIC one.** Chapter 1 told you **static testing matters more for performance than for functional testing because critical defects are introduced in architecture and design**; Chapter 3 told you risk analysis must be **started early and repeated regularly**; and the official rationale rewards exactly that combination. A single-technique answer — UI only, API only, network only — is almost always incomplete." } });
  B.push({ lead: "Related concepts: sequential development model, acceptance criteria at project outset, concept phase, requirements phase, analysis and design, coding/implementation, component testing, component integration testing, system testing, simulated interfaces, system integration testing, acceptance testing, verification versus validation, iterative and incremental models, Agile, dedicated performance iteration, separate performance team, Continuous Integration, regression testing, build stability, on-demand cloud test environment, test subsets, release planning, iteration planning, non-functional user stories, algorithm tuning, delivery monitoring, COTS, supplier/acquirer model, contractual acceptance criteria." });

  // ================= SECTION 2 =================
  B.push({ pagebreak: true });
  B.push({ h1: "Section 2 — Key Concepts" });
  B.push({ lead: "Concise, exam-ready notes drawn from the syllabus text. Every item is traceable to Chapter 3 of the CT-PT syllabus (pages 23–31)." });

  B.push({ h2: "2.1 The seven principal activities" });
  B.push({ ul: [
    "**Performance testing is iterative in nature** — each test's insights **correct or optimize application and system parameters**, and the next iteration **shows the results of modifications, until test objectives are reached**.",
    "**Performance testing activities align with the ISTQB test process.**",
    "**Test planning** — **particularly important** because of the **allocation of test environments, test data, tools and human resources**; **the scope of performance testing is established** here; **risk identification and risk analysis activities are completed** here; **risks, risk levels and risk status are modified** as conditions change.",
    "**Test monitoring and control** — **control measures define action plans** for **load generation capacity shortfalls · changed, new or replaced hardware · changes to network components · changes to software implementation**; **performance test objectives are evaluated to check for exit criteria achievement**.",
    "**Test analysis** — based on **performance requirements, test objectives, SLAs, IT architecture, process models and other test basis items**; may use **spreadsheets or capacity planning tools**; identifies **load levels, timing conditions and transactions to be tested**; **decides the required type(s) of performance test**.",
    "**Test design** — performance test cases are **generally created in modular form** as **building blocks of larger, more complex performance tests**.",
    "**Test implementation** — test cases are **ordered into performance test procedures** reflecting **the steps normally taken by the user**; **establishing and/or resetting the test environment before each execution**; **test data representative of actual production data in volume and type**.",
    "**Test execution** — the test is **conducted, often using performance test tools**; results **evaluated against requirements and objectives**; **defects reported**.",
    "**Test completion** — results provided to stakeholders (**architects, managers, product owners**) in a **test summary report**; metrics **often aggregated**; **dashboards** used; at **test closure**, **test cases, test procedures, test data and other testware are archived or passed on** for **system maintenance**.",
    "Performance testing is an **ongoing activity performed at multiple times and at all test levels**.",
  ] });

  B.push({ h2: "2.2 Nine architectures, nine risk profiles" });
  B.push({ ul: [
    "**Single computer** — **excessive resource consumption** (memory leaks, **background activities such as security software**, slow storage subsystems, **disk fragmentation**, **operating system mismanagement**) and **inefficient algorithms that do not use available resources**.",
    "**Multi-tier** — **poor or non-scalable database design · network bottlenecks · inadequate bandwidth or capacity on any single server** (plus all single-computer risks per server).",
    "**Distributed** — multi-tier risks plus **unreliable or unpredictable remote servers** with **periodic connection problems or intermittent intense load**; servers **may change dynamically**.",
    "**Virtualized** — **excessive load on the hardware across all the virtual machines**; **improper host VM configuration resulting in inadequate resources**.",
    "**Dynamic / cloud-based** — **self-scaling mitigates some risks**, but **failures to properly configure these features during initial setup or subsequent updates** remain.",
    "**Client-server** — client-side code carries **single-computer risks**; plus **connection speed and reliability**, **network congestion at the client connection point (public Wi-Fi)**, **firewalls, packet inspection and server load balancing**.",
    "**Mobile** — client-server and web-app risks plus **limited and variable resources and connectivity** (**location, battery life, charge state, available memory, temperature**), **slow dataflows from sensors and radios (accelerometers, Bluetooth)**, and **heavy interactions with other local apps and remote web services**.",
    "**Embedded real-time** — many mobile risks including **connectivity**, but **slowdowns can be catastrophic** (**vehicle braking**) rather than merely annoying.",
    "**Mainframe** — **predictable and fast as originally designed**, but now **accessible via APIs, web services or the database**, causing **unexpected loads that affect throughput of established applications**.",
    "**Any application may incorporate two or more architectures**, so **all relevant risks apply**; with **IoT and mobile**, **all architectures may be present and all risks can apply**.",
    "**Non-architectural technical decisions also create risk:** **C and C++ allow direct heap memory management → memory leaks** · **relational versus non-relational databases** · **recursive versus iterative algorithms**.",
  ] });

  B.push({ h2: "2.3 Performance risk across the lifecycle" });
  B.push({ ul: [
    "**Four steps:** **identify** risks focusing on **time behavior, resource utilization and capacity** → **assess**, ensuring **relevant architecture categories are addressed**, evaluating **likelihood and impact using clearly defined criteria** → **mitigate** based on **the nature of the risk item and the level of risk** → **manage on an ongoing basis** to ensure risks are **adequately mitigated prior to release**.",
    "**Both business and technical stakeholders** must participate. **Business stakeholders influence impact** (intended usage, business/societal/safety criticality, financial and reputational damage, civil or criminal legal liability). **Technical stakeholders influence likelihood** (requirements, architecture, design and implementation decisions).",
    "The process must have **the appropriate level of formality and rigor**, and must be **started early and repeated regularly**.",
    "**Do not rely entirely on performance testing conducted towards the end of system test and system integration test.**",
    "**Late discovery of performance defects** — from **requirements, design, architecture and implementation decisions made early** — has caused **unfortunate surprises**, especially in **larger and more complex systems of systems projects**.",
    "**Example:** **many-to-many joins** on a poorly designed relational database may only appear **during dynamic testing with large-scale test datasets at system test**, but **a careful technical review including experienced database engineers can predict the problems prior to database implementation**.",
    "**Mitigation must span the entire software development process, not just dynamic testing** — e.g., when transaction volumes or simultaneous-user counts **cannot be specified early**, **design and architecture decisions should allow for highly variable scalability (on-demand cloud-based computing resources)**.",
    "**Residual risk always exists** and must be given a **realistic and specific evaluation**. \"**Yes, it's still possible for customers to experience long delays**\" is unhelpful; **the percentage of customers likely to experience delays equal to or exceeding certain thresholds** is helpful.",
  ] });

  B.push({ h2: "2.4 Lifecycle models" });
  B.push({ ul: [
    "**Sequential — ideal practice:** **include performance criteria as part of the acceptance criteria defined at the outset of the project**; **each successive performance test activity is based on items defined in the prior activities**.",
    "**Nine sequential phases:** **Concept** (verify performance goals are defined as acceptance criteria) · **Requirements** (verify requirements are defined and represent stakeholder needs correctly) · **Analysis and Design** (verify the design reflects the requirements) · **Coding/Implementation** (verify the code is efficient and reflects requirements and design) · **Component Testing** · **Component Integration Testing** · **System Testing** (**hardware, software, procedures and data representative of production**; **interfaces may be simulated if they give a true representation of performance**) · **System Integration Testing** (**the entire system, representative of production**) · **Acceptance Testing** (**validate against the originally stated user needs and acceptance criteria**).",
    "**Iterative / incremental:** performance testing **can occur as part of the first iteration, or as an iteration dedicated entirely to performance testing**; **execution may be performed by a separate team**.",
    "**CI:** most common objective is **regression testing and build stability**; performance tests can join CI **if designed to be executed at a build level**. **Four extra concerns:** **on-demand test environment (cloud)** · **which tests to automate, given the short CI timeframe (a subset of more extensive tests run by a specialist team)** · **creating tests whose main objective is to ensure a change does not negatively impact performance** · **executing on portions of the system, requiring rapid testing and selectable subsets**.",
    "**Seven iterative activities:** **Release Planning** (all iterations in a release; risks identified and assessed; final pre-release testing planned) · **Iteration Planning** (risks assessed **in more detail for each user story**) · **User Story Creation** (**\"non-functional\" user stories**; criteria in the **acceptance criteria**) · **Design of performance tests** · **Coding/Implementation** (**component-level testing, e.g., tuning algorithms**) · **Testing/Evaluation** (may be a **separate activity** or a **dedicated iteration**; wider scope for a completed set of stories) · **Delivery** (**monitor performance in the production environment**).",
    "**COTS / supplier-acquirer:** testing is needed **from both supplier and acquirer perspectives**; **it is often the customer's responsibility to validate that performance meets their requirements**; for **customized vendor-developed software**, criteria **should be specified in the contract**; for **COTS**, **the customer has sole responsibility to test in a realistic test environment prior to deployment**.",
  ] });

  B.push({ h2: "2.5 Chapter 3 keyword glossary" });
  B.push({ p: "The syllabus lists **four keywords** for Chapter 3: **metric, risk, software development lifecycle, test log**. All are examinable at **K1** even where no learning objective names them." });
  B.push({ table: { head: ["Keyword", "Definition to memorise"], widths: [3.4, 10.6], rows: [
    ["**Metric**", "A defined, context-qualified measure used to evaluate performance. In Chapter 3 metrics appear at **test completion**, where results are **expressed through metrics which are often aggregated to simplify the meaning of the test results**, and reported via **dashboards**. _(syllabus keyword)_"],
    ["**Risk**", "A potential problem to product quality — for performance, focused on **time behavior, resource utilization and capacity** — evaluated in terms of **likelihood and impact using clearly defined criteria**. **Business stakeholders influence impact; technical stakeholders influence likelihood.** _(syllabus keyword)_"],
    ["**Software development lifecycle**", "The model that determines how performance testing activities are **organized and performed** — **sequential**, **iterative and incremental**, or **COTS / supplier-acquirer**. _(syllabus keyword)_"],
    ["**Test log**", "The record of each test run. Defined in detail in **PTFL-4.4.1**: **date/time of test start · test duration · scripts used (including script mix) and configuration data · test data files used · name and location of data/log files created · HW/SW configuration tested (especially changes between runs) · average and peak CPU and RAM utilization on web and database servers · notes on achieved performance · defects identified.** _(syllabus keyword)_"],
    ["**Residual risk**", "The risk that remains after mitigation. **It always exists**, and must be communicated as a **realistic and specific evaluation** — ideally as **the percentage of customers likely to experience delays equal to or exceeding certain thresholds**."],
    ["**Test summary report**", "The deliverable of **test completion**, in which **performance test results are provided to the stakeholders** such as **architects, managers and product owners**."],
    ["**Non-functional user story**", "In Agile, a user story whose **specific performance criteria are described in the associated acceptance criteria**."],
    ["**Point of test closure**", "The stage at which **designed tests, test tool assets (test cases and test procedures), test data and other testware are archived or passed on to other testers for later use during system maintenance activities**."],
  ] } });

